# Linux kernel coding style for the six genuine kernel modules listed under
# "kernel_style" in tools/c_style_manifest.json.
#
# Derived from Documentation/process/coding-style.rst at Linux v7.0 (sections
# 1-3: 8-wide tab indentation, 80 preferred columns, K&R braces with the
# function brace on its own line, no braces for single statements). The
# upstream .clang-format could not be retrieved during this revision (HTTP 429
# and 502 on 2026-10-29); this is a minimal equivalent, not that file.
---
# Options shared by every language section below (clang-format applies a
# section without Language to all languages).
BasedOnStyle: LLVM
IndentWidth: 8
TabWidth: 8
ContinuationIndentWidth: 8
UseTab: Always
ColumnLimit: 80
LineEnding: LF
BreakBeforeBraces: Linux
InsertBraces: false
AllowShortBlocksOnASingleLine: Never
AllowShortFunctionsOnASingleLine: None
AllowShortIfStatementsOnASingleLine: Never
AllowShortLoopsOnASingleLine: false
AllowShortCaseLabelsOnASingleLine: false
AllowShortEnumsOnASingleLine: false
IndentCaseLabels: false
IndentGotoLabels: false
PointerAlignment: Right
DerivePointerAlignment: false
SpaceBeforeParens: ControlStatements
SpaceAfterCStyleCast: false
AlignConsecutiveMacros: None
AlignEscapedNewlines: Left
SortIncludes: Never
IncludeBlocks: Preserve
BreakStringLiterals: false
ReflowComments: IndentOnly
QualifierAlignment: Leave
---
# .c files are parsed as C, so C++ keywords such as `protected` or `new`
# are ordinary identifiers. Headers (.h, .inc) are detected as C++ and use
# the same options.
Language: C
---
Language: Cpp
...
